course: dictionary
lesson: 13641
title: Things that are certain to happen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Inevitable is a long word, but you can work out its meaning from the clues around it. Inevitable means certain to happen, so it cannot be avoided or stopped. If it is inevitable that the Sun will set tonight, nobody can stop it.
explain: «The rain was inevitable, so Mia took her coat to sports day.» Mia knew the rain would come, and nothing could stop it. Ask yourself: is this thing sure to happen? If it is, inevitable fits. Swap in your meaning, "certain to happen", and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Inevitable can also mean something so usual that everyone expects it. «The inevitable question was: are we there yet?» Nobody was surprised, because everyone knew the question was coming.
explain: Other forms of the word help too. «Inevitably, the match ended in a draw» uses the adverb inevitably. «He tried to delay the inevitable» means he put off something that was going to happen anyway.
fact: «Inevitable» means certain to happen and impossible to avoid or stop.
fact: «Inevitably» means in a way that cannot be avoided: «Inevitably, the ice cream melted in the sun.»
fact: In science and maths, an inevitable outcome is certain: a dropped ball will fall to the ground.
fact: «The inevitable» is a noun phrase meaning the thing that is bound to happen.
fact: Informally, inevitable can mean so usual that everyone expects it, like «the inevitable rain on sports day».
exercise: «The Sun will set this evening; it is inevitable.» What does inevitable mean here? | Happening only in summer | Very bright and golden | Something that might happen, but probably won't | *Certain to happen and impossible to stop
exercise: Which of these is an inevitable event? | A jump that could be stopped halfway | *A dropped ball falling to the ground | A cat that could talk | A lottery prize you might win
exercise: «The inevitable question came at the start of the car journey.» What does the inevitable question mean? | A question that was never asked | A question about dinner | A question written in a book | *A question everyone knew was coming
test: «It was inevitable that the bus would be late in the snow.» What does inevitable mean? | Bright and shiny | *Certain to happen | Very fast | Possible but unlikely
test: Which word is the adverb in «Inevitably, the picnic was rained off»? | Off | Picnic | Rained | *Inevitably
exam: Mr Okoro says, «Delay the inevitable» means what? | Stop something from ever happening | *Put off something that is certain to happen | Ask someone to hurry up | Make a loud noise
